22:["$","$L34",null,{"faqs":[{"question":"What is legacy system modernization?","answer":"Legacy system modernization is updating outdated software, platforms, and infrastructure — from mainframes and old codebases to unsupported applications — into modern, maintainable systems. The goal is to run faster, integrate with modern tools, cut maintenance cost, and stop depending on aging technology and the shrinking pool of people who can support it."},{"question":"What are the approaches to legacy modernization?","answer":"The classic options (the '7 R's') simplify to three: rehost (lift-and-shift onto modern infrastructure, fastest), replatform/refactor (update frameworks, databases, and APIs while keeping the business logic — where most systems belong), and rebuild (re-architect cloud-native the parts that earn it). We pick per component, not for the whole system."},{"question":"Should we modernize or rebuild from scratch?","answer":"Usually modernize. A from-scratch rebuild is slow, expensive, and risky, and you lose years of business logic encoded in the old system. We keep what works, update incrementally, and rebuild only the components that truly earn it — far lower risk than a big-bang replacement."},{"question":"How do you integrate legacy systems with modern platforms?","answer":"We wrap the legacy system in APIs, so modern apps, cloud services, and AI can talk to it through a stable boundary. Then we modernize behind that boundary, piece by piece, while the old system keeps running and retires gradually — no all-at-once cutover."},{"question":"How much does legacy modernization cost?","answer":"It depends on the size and complexity of the system and how much you rebuild. Senior engineering starts at $50 USD/hour, and we scope a fixed-price assessment first so you see the plan and cost before any change. Modernization usually pays back in lower maintenance cost, fewer scarce specialists, and faster delivery."},{"question":"How do you modernize without breaking the business?","answer":"Incrementally. We assess and map dependencies first, wrap the legacy in APIs, and modernize in stages — each with validation and a rollback plan — so the business keeps running throughout. There's no big-bang cutover where one failure halts operations."}],"id":"faq","title":"What teams ask before modernizing.","fill":true}]
